@AndrewKushnir thanks for looking into it! Good to know that Codelyzer is synced into g3 and compiled from source.

So yes, this is a breaking change, however all of the `@angular/compiler` surface is not considered public. Therefore, I don't think we should go through the hoops of introducing a new, strict, visitor interface, as it will complicate stuff quite a bit (mostly because `AstVisitor` is both [API and SPI](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2954372/difference-between-spi-and-api))

We could either 1) get Codelyzer patched through a CL now, or 2) pull out the bug fix into its own PR, without the visitor changes. The introduction of strict types can then be delayed until `master` corresponds with `9.0` development. There's more area for improvement on the typing requirements of visitors, so maybe all of those can be done in a single PR.

Either way, once the strict types are introduced there needs to be a patch CL that updates Codelyzer together with this change, and then update Codelyzer itself (on GH) once the first `.next` release is available. This is always a requirement for such changes to compiler code, as there's a catch-22 with Codelyzer being compiled from source in g3.

Besides the type errors that occur when compiling from source, there's a runtime breaking change in terms of the call to `AstVisitor.visitEmptyExpr`. We can guard this call to not break older consumers, and remove the guard sometime in the future (that would be v11 or so).

```typescript
class EmptyExpr extends AST {
  visit<R>(visitor: AstVisitor<R>, context: any): R {
    // Provides backwards compatibility for older visitors. This is not allowed by the typings,
    // but may occur for consumers that are compiled against an Angular version prior to 8.2/9.0
    if (visitor.visitEmptyExpr === undefined) {
      // The `undefined` return type is not allowed according to the typings, however this
      // is what would have been returned prior to 8.2/9.0 so such older consumers have
      // always had to deal with `undefined` values.
      return undefined !;
    }
    return visitor.visitEmptyExpr(this, context);
  }
}
```

Note that `AstVisitor.visitEmptyExpr` is not typed to be optional, as that would introduce type-safety risks as we have them today.
